WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.180
They're iconic.

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:02.270
They're memorable.

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:04.270
And they were never meant to happen.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:09.430
Sometimes, an ingenious on-set improvisation
creates a moment that not even a film's directors

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:14.950
and screenwriters could capture, and unexpectedly
cements itself as an unquestionable classic.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:19.060
Though they were never in the script, here
are a few memorable movie scenes that just

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.539
kind of… happened.

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:22.930
Caddyshack

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:27.109
One of the most popular comedies in history,
it isn't hard to understand why Caddyshack

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:31.900
is a classic, especially with a cast that
included Chevy Chase, Rodney Dangerfield and

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:32.900
Bill Murray.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:37.500
The film had a chaotic production, thanks
to the cast's penchant for constantly ad-libbing

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.440
their lines, but Murray was easily the worst
offender.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:46.700
In what's become known among fans as the "Cinderella"
scene, Murray, as caddy Carl Spackler, fantasizes

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.399
about life as a professional golfer — and
the rest is comedy history.

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:56.680
"Somebody know where, he's got about 350 yards,
he's gonna hit about 500 yards, don't ya think?

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.129
He's got a beautiful back swing.

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:00.129
That's!

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:01.379
Oh, he got out of that one!"

00:01:01.379 --> 00:01:05.779
According to director Harold Ramis, the improv
ran almost 30 minutes, which is something

00:01:05.779 --> 00:01:08.280
we'd all love to see in a Director's Cut.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.679
Unfortunately, it'll probably never happen.

00:01:10.679 --> 00:01:11.729
"I want ---"

00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:14.249
"You'll get nothing and like it!"

00:01:14.249 --> 00:01:15.909
Dazed and Confused

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:20.119
Matthew McConaughey has built an impressive
filmography over the years, but no matter

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:24.840
how many awards he wins, he'll never escape
the three words he uttered in 1993.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.380
"Alright, alright, alright!"

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.719
It's become one of the signature lines from
Dazed and Confused, and McConaughey improvised

00:01:33.719 --> 00:01:34.719
it on the spot.

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:39.099
The actor claims to have based the line on
a live album performance of "Roadhouse Blues"

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:40.099
by The Doors.

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:43.999
"I'd been listening to this live, Morrison
live Doors album.

00:01:43.999 --> 00:01:49.819
And in between two of the songs, Morrison
goes, 'Alright, alright, alright!'"

00:01:49.819 --> 00:01:54.079
The line became a catchphrase on set and in
pop culture, and it's since become one of

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:56.640
McConaughey's signature lines in real life.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.260
"And to that I say, 'Alright, alright, alright!'"

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.360
All right.

00:02:02.029 --> 00:02:04.539
Pirates of the Caribbean: Dead Man's Chest

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:09.039
The story of Dead Man's Chest picks up with
Jack Sparrow doing battle with the nefarious

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:14.050
dead pirate Davy Jones, and as he's done in
every Pirates movie, Depp gave a charming,

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.430
bizarre performance.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:19.959
One scene had Jack parading around with a
jar of dirt, mocking Jones and confusing his

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:20.959
fellow crewmates.

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:22.739
"I've got a jar of dirt.

00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:23.950
I've got a jar of dirt!

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:26.220
And guess what's inside it?!"

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.020
"Enough!"

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:33.080
Before filming, Depp asked director Gore Verbinski
if he could improvise, and you don't really

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.670
say no to Johnny Depp.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:39.069
Depp began to dance around with the jar while
the cameras rolled, and Verbinski caught the

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:42.260
genuinely perplexed reaction of the other
actors.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:47.330
But really, after so much time on set with
Depp, nothing should have been too surprising.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.330
Blade Runner

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:53.840
Ridley Scott's sci-fi opus Blade Runner notoriously
suffered from a troubled production, which

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.019
included an ever-changing script that pushed
the cost of the film ever higher.

00:02:58.019 --> 00:03:02.610
One of the movie's best lines, however, didn't
come from the screenwriters, but actor Rutger

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:03.610
Hauer.

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:07.319
Roy spends the movie trying desperately to
find a "cure" for the design flaw dooming

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:11.980
him and his friends to shortened lives, but
by the end of the film, he's given up hope.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.480
As he's slowly dying, he recalls a short list
of incredible things he's seen in his short

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:21.430
life, which is partially improvised and altered
by Hauer himself from the original screenplay's

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:23.190
30 or so lines.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:27.200
Hauer and Scott tend to disagree about who
added what and where, but both agree that

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.050
the film's most iconic line was 100-percent,
pure, on-the-spot Hauer.

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:44.700
"All those moments will be lost in time, like
tears… in rain."

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:49.639
Also not in the script, according to Hauer's
autobiography, was the entire chase scene,

00:03:49.639 --> 00:03:53.989
which was originally a fight in a gym, and
the dove that he's holding as he dies, both

00:03:53.989 --> 00:03:57.779
ideas that Hauer himself introduced to the
story.

00:03:57.779 --> 00:03:59.459
Big

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:03.219
It's the story of a 12-year-old boy who makes
a wish to be a grownup.

00:04:03.219 --> 00:04:07.450
Against all odds, the wish comes true, and
the man-with-a-12-year-old's-brain, played

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.750
by Tom Hanks, realizes he has a lot to learn
about living an adult life.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:16.549
And one of those things you have to learn
as a man is how to properly eat unusual foods.

00:04:16.549 --> 00:04:21.389
Hanks got caught up in acting like an oblivious
preteen, and improvised the whole thing.

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:24.870
Director Penny Marshall found it hilarious,
and kept the take in the final cut.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.550
Full Metal Jacket

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:32.080
Director Stanley Kubrick was known for his
overwhelmingly meticulous demands of his actors,

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.490
and his 1987 war drama Full Metal Jacket was
no exception.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:41.430
Former drill instructor R. Lee Ermey joined
the production as a technical advisor at Stanley

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:45.680
Kubrick's request, but once on board, he expressed
his interest in the role of drill Sergeant

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:46.680
Hartman.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:51.051
When Kubrick saw a video of Ermey insulting
a group of potential actors, he cast him as

00:04:51.051 --> 00:04:52.280
Sergeant Hartman on the spot.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:56.389
"You're so ugly, you could be a modern art
masterpiece."

00:04:56.389 --> 00:05:00.780
In a most unusual move for the controlling
Kubrick, Ermey was allowed to improvise most

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.669
of his own dialogue, in particular the insults
that made the character so memorable.

00:05:04.669 --> 00:05:09.349
"Your ass looks like about 150 pounds of chewed
bubble gum, you know that, Pyle?"

00:05:09.349 --> 00:05:10.349
"Sir, yes, sir!"

00:05:10.349 --> 00:05:14.990
Full Metal Jacket won critical raves when
it opened, especially for Ermey's performance.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:17.900
He's since gone on to a prestigious acting
career.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.770
Mostly screaming at people.

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.389
The Departed

00:05:21.389 --> 00:05:25.550
While filming The Departed, Jack Nicholson
often approached director Martin Scorsese

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:29.889
with ideas about his character, a violent
mobster naked Frank Costello.

00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:35.380
In one scene, Nicholson totally surprised
co-star Leonardo DiCaprio in the worst way.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.100
"You got something you wanna... ask me?"

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:45.050
Nicholson had proposed the interaction to
Martin Scorsese, who allowed Nicholson to

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:49.490
pull a real, unscripted gun on DiCaprio, who
reacted with genuine fear.

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:54.129
A little because having an unknown gun waved
in your face is scary, but more because Nicholson

00:05:54.129 --> 00:05:58.270
was making every effort to be actively terrifying
on set.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:05:59.819
Saving Private Ryan

00:05:59.819 --> 00:06:04.970
Directed by Steven Spielberg, Saving Private
Ryan follows the adventures of an Army squad

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:07.970
in search of the title character during World
War II.

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:12.169
Matt Damon, who had just scored an Oscar nomination
for his performance in Good Will Hunting,

00:06:12.169 --> 00:06:14.169
was cast for one of the main roles.

00:06:14.169 --> 00:06:18.639
At one point, Ryan tells a pretty odd story
to his squad captain about catching his brother

00:06:18.639 --> 00:06:20.669
gettin' busy with a questionable girl.

00:06:20.669 --> 00:06:26.690
"Picture a girl who took a nose dive from
the Ugly Tree and hit every branch comin'

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:27.690
down!"

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:31.440
Matt Damon, an Academy Award-winning writer,
improvised the story.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.480
The crew thought Damon had botched it with
the weird, meandering story that didn't really

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:36.480
have a point.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:40.540
Spielberg, however, thought it fit the character
perfectly and kept it in the movie.

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:44.580
Because who doesn't love a story about watching
your sibling doing the nasty?

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:45.580
Thanks for watching!

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:48.420
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.070
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

